Revision history for OCP

{{$NEXT}}
    - Drift detection (OCP::Drift)
      - compares running component versions against the version manifest
      - compares kubelet versions against the configured distribution
      - compares publicIp pinned in ocp.yaml against the recorded status
      - ocp status reports drift, ocp apply runs the upgrade step for what
        can be fixed automatically instead of dying
    - ocp kubeconfig -e merges into $KUBECONFIG or ~/.kube/config
      - renames cluster/user/context from 'default' to the cluster name
      - keeps entries of other clusters, backs up the previous file
      - apply no longer writes a plaintext .kube/config into the project
    - Clean error messages: no Perl stacktraces, --verbose for the raw error
    - Fix ocp version and ocp update: OCP::Config was missing status,
      cluster_status, set_status and save_status
    - Fix ocp node/provider subcommands: MooX::Cmd looked for them under
      ::Cmd, so 'ocp node ls' never dispatched
    - Fix ocp update option specs (format => '' is not valid)
    - Accept 'ocp deploy-robocop' and 'ocp inject-key' spellings
    - Accept a positional NAME for ocp node add/rm
    - Fix OCP::Node->teardown passing the node name where the provider id
      belongs, which left Hetzner servers running after ocp node rm
    - Share provider behaviour via OCP::Role::Provider::ExistingHost; the
      local provider now runs its commands without SSH
    - Remove Traefik leftovers (replaced by Cilium Gateway API)
    - Remove Tickit/TUI leftovers from Dockerfile and cpanfile
    - kubectl is back in the Docker image as a debugging tool
    - Add shared OCP::Kubernetes typed helper for cluster access
      - centralizes Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig setup for OCP commands
      - provides reusable node summary helpers for typed Node objects
      - keeps optional IO::K8s provider registration in one place
    - Refactor ocp status to use typed Kubernetes API access
      - removes kubectl as the primary status data path
      - formats node summaries via shared Perl helpers
      - improves groundwork for moving more commands off shell-driven K8s access
    - Core Registry: Pull-through cache (docker.io) + local registry
      - ocp-cache (NodePort 30500) caches all Docker Hub pulls
      - ocp-registry (NodePort 30501) for user images
      - RKE2 registries.yaml auto-configured on all nodes
      - Deployed first after node Ready (all subsequent pulls cached)
    - Component reconciliation with hash tracking
      - ocp apply on existing clusters checks/deploys missing components
      - .ocp/deployed.yaml tracks MD5 of deployed manifests
      - Changed manifests (new OCP version) trigger re-deploy
    - Complete NVIDIA GPU support for Kubernetes
      - Unified install_nvidia task (replaces separate Hetzner/generic)
      - All required packages: nvidia-driver, nvidia-smi, libcuda1, nvidia-container-toolkit
      - Kernel headers for running kernel (prevents DKMS mismatch)
      - Verification: module loaded, nvidia-smi, libcuda, container-toolkit
      - containerd nvidia runtime configured before RKE2 start (no restart needed)
      - RuntimeClass + Device Plugin auto-deployed by ocp apply
      - GPU pods work with runtimeClassName: nvidia + nvidia.com/gpu resource
    - Cilium Gateway API (replaces Traefik)
      - Gateway with HTTP/HTTPS listeners
      - LB-IPAM for bare-metal LoadBalancer support
      - CiliumL2AnnouncementPolicy for external IP
    - Fix SSH host key verification (switch from Net::SSH2 to Net::OpenSSH)
      - Net::SSH2 (libssh2) silently ignores StrictHostKeyChecking setting
      - Unknown hosts no longer cause "Couldn't authenticate" errors
    - AMD GPU: Disabled for now (ROCm not available on Debian)
      - AMD dropped Debian from ROCm packages (only Ubuntu/RHEL/SLES)
      - ROCm userspace belongs in container images, not on host
      - detect_gpu still detects AMD but skips driver installation
    - RKE2 + K3s dual distribution support via Rex framework
    - Cilium CNI installation (kube-proxy replacement, eBPF)
    - Robust GPU detection with CUDA compute capability check
      - PCI class codes [0300]/[0302] + vendor IDs [10de]/[1002]
      - NVIDIA compute check: only installs for CUDA-capable GPUs
      - Whitelisted: RTX, GTX 10xx+, Quadro, Tesla, datacenter (A/H/L/V/P)
      - Skips: GeForce MX (laptop), GT/GTS/NVS (legacy), pre-Pascal
      - Virtual GPU detection (virtio/QEMU/VMware/VirtualBox)
    - Single-node mode (control plane untainting)
    - Rex-based server automation (share/Rexfile)
    - Robocop Kubernetes controller for CRD-based node management
      - OCPNode and OCPNodeProvider CRDs
      - Net::Async::Kubernetes based reconciliation loop
      - In-cluster controller deployment
    - Development registry support (ocp dev command)
      - Build robocop image from source
      - In-cluster registry deployment
      - Auto-update controller deployments
    - Local provider with auto-detection
      - Docker mode: SSH to localhost with clear messaging
      - Native mode: Direct installation via OCP::Local
      - Automatic environment detection (/.dockerenv)
    - CLI commands: init, apply, status, destroy, kubeconfig, hetzner, dev
    - Hetzner Cloud provider support via WWW::Hetzner
    - SSH provider for existing servers (control planes + workers)
    - Pure Perl secrets management via Crypt::Age and File::SOPS
    - Spec/Status separation (ocp.yaml vs .ocp/status.yaml)
    - Age encryption for secrets (no external tools required)
    - SSH key generation and management
    - Clean error messages (no Perl stacktraces)
